                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("d3100000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What is the passage mainly about?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Why rural areas need paved roads\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"How trees cool cities and what supports their success\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Why cities should stop planting trees\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"How to measure the age of a tree\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The passage combines cooling mechanisms, limitations, and long-term planting design.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"How does evaporation help cool the air?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It prevents all rainfall\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It releases stored solar energy\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It takes heat from the air\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It increases road temperature\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The first paragraph explains that evaporating water takes heat from surrounding air.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"The word \\u0027constraints\\u0027 is closest in meaning to\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"celebrations\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"measurements\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"predictions\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"limitations\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"The phrase refers to conditions affecting success, such as water needs and climate.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"Why does the author mention permeable paving?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To give an example of supporting tree survival\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To argue that shade is unnecessary\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To describe a rural farming method\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To show that roots should remain dry\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"Permeable surfaces illustrate planning trees as part of the surrounding system.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"What can be inferred about counting newly planted trees?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It always measures cooling accurately\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It is sufficient for planning\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It does not alone show long-term benefits\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It proves that trees will survive\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The final paragraph emphasizes conditions for survival rather than the number planted.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 1,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Cities are often warmer than nearby rural areas because roads and buildings absorb solar energy and release it slowly. Trees can reduce this local warming in two ways. Their leaves shade surfaces, preventing some energy from reaching the ground. Trees also release water through their leaves; as this water evaporates, it takes heat from the surrounding air.\n\nThe benefits are unevenly distributed. A single tree beside a wide road provides less continuous shade than a connected row of trees. Moreover, a species that grows quickly may require more water than the local climate can supply. Planting without considering these constraints can lead to trees dying before they provide substantial shade.\n\nFor this reason, some urban planners combine tree planting with changes to the surfaces beneath them. Permeable paving allows rainfall to reach roots instead of running directly into drains. Such planning treats trees as part of a larger system. The aim is not merely to increase the number planted, but to create conditions in which they can survive and cool streets for decades.",
                            Title = "Urban trees and local temperatures",
                            Version = 1
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("d3200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What is the passage mainly about?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Why rural areas need paved roads\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"How trees cool cities and what supports their success\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Why cities should stop planting trees\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"How to measure the age of a tree\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The passage combines cooling mechanisms, limitations, and long-term planting design.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"How does evaporation help cool the air?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It prevents all rainfall\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It releases stored solar energy\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It takes heat from the air\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It increases road temperature\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The first paragraph explains that evaporating water takes heat from surrounding air.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"The word \\u0027constraints\\u0027 is closest in meaning to\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"celebrations\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"measurements\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"predictions\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"limitations\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"The phrase refers to conditions affecting success, such as water needs and climate.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"Why does the author mention permeable paving?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To give an example of supporting tree survival\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To argue that shade is unnecessary\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To describe a rural farming method\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To show that roots should remain dry\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"Permeable surfaces illustrate planning trees as part of the surrounding system.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"What can be inferred about counting newly planted trees?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It always measures cooling accurately\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It is sufficient for planning\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It does not alone show long-term benefits\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It proves that trees will survive\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The final paragraph emphasizes conditions for survival rather than the number planted.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 1,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Cities are often warmer than nearby rural areas because roads and buildings absorb solar energy and release it slowly. Trees can reduce this local warming in two ways. Their leaves shade surfaces, preventing some energy from reaching the ground. Trees also release water through their leaves; as this water evaporates, it takes heat from the surrounding air.\n\nThe benefits are unevenly distributed. A single tree beside a wide road provides less continuous shade than a connected row of trees. Moreover, a species that grows quickly may require more water than the local climate can supply. Planting without considering these constraints can lead to trees dying before they provide substantial shade.\n\nFor this reason, some urban planners combine tree planting with changes to the surfaces beneath them. Permeable paving allows rainfall to reach roots instead of running directly into drains. Such planning treats trees as part of a larger system. The aim is not merely to increase the number planted, but to create conditions in which they can survive and cool streets for decades.",
                            Title = "Urban trees and local temperatures",
                            Version = 2
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("d3100000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What distinction is central to the passage?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Printed versus digital notes\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Familiarity versus unaided recall\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Morning versus evening study\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Science versus history lessons\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The first paragraph distinguishes familiarity from recalling without help.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"Why should learners check answers after retrieval?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To avoid all future effort\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To memorize page numbers\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To identify and correct gaps\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To make sessions longer\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"Comparing an answer with its source reveals gaps and errors.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"What does \\u0027accessible\\u0027 mean in the final paragraph?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Available to recall\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Printed in large type\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Free to purchase\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Located nearby\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The phrase describes information remaining available in memory over time.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"What can be inferred about an easy study session?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It must involve retrieval\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It guarantees long-term learning\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It requires no feedback\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It may give a misleading impression of learning\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"Ease and familiarity alone do not demonstrate lasting learning.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"How is the second paragraph organized?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"A historical event followed by dates\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"A benefit followed by a condition for its success\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Two unrelated definitions\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"A list of examination rules\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The author explains the benefit of identifying gaps, then adds the need for accurate feedback.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 2,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Students often prepare for an examination by reading their notes repeatedly. This can make the material feel familiar, but familiarity is not the same as being able to recall an idea without help. An alternative approach asks students to close their notes and attempt to explain what they remember. This process is known as retrieval practice.\n\nRetrieval can be useful even when the first attempt is incomplete. After answering, a learner checks the original material and identifies gaps. The comparison provides a clearer guide for further study than the general feeling that a page looks familiar. However, feedback matters: repeatedly producing an incorrect answer without checking it may strengthen the error rather than correct it.\n\nSpacing these attempts over several days adds another challenge. Some forgetting occurs between sessions, so recall requires effort again. Although this can feel less comfortable than immediate repetition, it helps reveal whether knowledge remains accessible over time. Effective study therefore involves both attempting to retrieve information and using reliable feedback to refine it, rather than judging success solely by how easy a study session feels.",
                            Title = "When retrieval improves learning",
                            Version = 1
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("d3200000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002"),
