                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What distinction is central to the passage?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Printed versus digital notes\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Familiarity versus unaided recall\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Morning versus evening study\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Science versus history lessons\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The first paragraph distinguishes familiarity from recalling without help.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"Why should learners check answers after retrieval?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To avoid all future effort\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To memorize page numbers\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To identify and correct gaps\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To make sessions longer\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"Comparing an answer with its source reveals gaps and errors.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"What does \\u0027accessible\\u0027 mean in the final paragraph?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Available to recall\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Printed in large type\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Free to purchase\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Located nearby\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The phrase describes information remaining available in memory over time.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"What can be inferred about an easy study session?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It must involve retrieval\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It guarantees long-term learning\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It requires no feedback\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It may give a misleading impression of learning\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"Ease and familiarity alone do not demonstrate lasting learning.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"How is the second paragraph organized?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"A historical event followed by dates\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"A benefit followed by a condition for its success\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Two unrelated definitions\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"A list of examination rules\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The author explains the benefit of identifying gaps, then adds the need for accurate feedback.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 2,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Students often prepare for an examination by reading their notes repeatedly. This can make the material feel familiar, but familiarity is not the same as being able to recall an idea without help. An alternative approach asks students to close their notes and attempt to explain what they remember. This process is known as retrieval practice.\n\nRetrieval can be useful even when the first attempt is incomplete. After answering, a learner checks the original material and identifies gaps. The comparison provides a clearer guide for further study than the general feeling that a page looks familiar. However, feedback matters: repeatedly producing an incorrect answer without checking it may strengthen the error rather than correct it.\n\nSpacing these attempts over several days adds another challenge. Some forgetting occurs between sessions, so recall requires effort again. Although this can feel less comfortable than immediate repetition, it helps reveal whether knowledge remains accessible over time. Effective study therefore involves both attempting to retrieve information and using reliable feedback to refine it, rather than judging success solely by how easy a study session feels.",
                            Title = "When retrieval improves learning",
                            Version = 2
