:\"To claim scientists should use diaries only\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To emphasize that interpretation involves uncertainty\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To argue that deeper layers are always younger\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To dismiss historical records\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"Uncertainty about dates and causes prevents reading sediment as a precise diary.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 5,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Material gradually settles on the bottoms of lakes, forming layers of sediment. These layers may contain pollen, mineral particles, and remains of small organisms. By removing a narrow column of sediment, researchers can examine a sequence in which deeper material is generally older than material near the surface. The column acts as a partial record of the surrounding environment.\n\nPollen offers clues about plants that grew nearby. If pollen from trees becomes less common while grass pollen increases, the landscape may have become more open. However, the change could reflect either a shift in climate or human activities such as clearing land. A single indicator rarely identifies the cause with certainty.\n\nResearchers strengthen interpretations by comparing pollen with other evidence, including charcoal particles and historical records. Charcoal may indicate burning, though it does not reveal by itself whether the fire was natural or deliberately started. Establishing the age of each layer also involves uncertainty. Rather than treating sediment as a precise diary, scientists combine several lines of evidence to build and revise explanations of environmental change over time.",
