                            Id = new Guid("6315129c-ce48-f6fe-a506-d3be04758083"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What is the passage mainly about?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Why rural areas need paved roads\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"How trees cool cities and what supports their success\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Why cities should stop planting trees\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"How to measure the age of a tree\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The passage combines cooling mechanisms, limitations, and long-term planting design.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"How does evaporation help cool the air?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It prevents all rainfall\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It releases stored solar energy\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It takes heat from the air\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It increases road temperature\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The first paragraph explains that evaporating water takes heat from surrounding air.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"The word \\u0027constraints\\u0027 is closest in meaning to\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"celebrations\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"measurements\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"predictions\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"limitations\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"The phrase refers to conditions affecting success, such as water needs and climate.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"Why does the author mention permeable paving?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To give an example of supporting tree survival\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To argue that shade is unnecessary\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To describe a rural farming method\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To show that roots should remain dry\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"Permeable surfaces illustrate planning trees as part of the surrounding system.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"What can be inferred about counting newly planted trees?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It always measures cooling accurately\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It is sufficient for planning\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It does not alone show long-term benefits\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It proves that trees will survive\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The final paragraph emphasizes conditions for survival rather than the number planted.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 1,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Cities are often warmer than nearby rural areas because roads and buildings absorb solar energy and release it slowly. Trees can reduce this local warming in two ways. Their leaves shade surfaces, preventing some energy from reaching the ground. Trees also release water through their leaves; as this water evaporates, it takes heat from the surrounding air.\n\nThe benefits are unevenly distributed. A single tree beside a wide road provides less continuous shade than a connected row of trees. Moreover, a species that grows quickly may require more water than the local climate can supply. Planting without considering these constraints can lead to trees dying before they provide substantial shade.\n\nFor this reason, some urban planners combine tree planting with changes to the surfaces beneath them. Permeable paving allows rainfall to reach roots instead of running directly into drains. Such planning treats trees as part of a larger system. The aim is not merely to increase the number planted, but to create conditions in which they can survive and cool streets for decades.",
                            Title = "Urban trees and local temperatures",
                            Version = 3
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("eb7aaaa9-007d-5974-fd3d-e2245160047b"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What distinction is central to the passage?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Printed versus digital notes\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Familiarity versus unaided recall\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Morning versus evening study\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Science versus history lessons\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The first paragraph distinguishes familiarity from recalling without help.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"Why should learners check answers after retrieval?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To avoid all future effort\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To memorize page numbers\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To identify and correct gaps\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To make sessions longer\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"Comparing an answer with its source reveals gaps and errors.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"What does \\u0027accessible\\u0027 mean in the final paragraph?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Available to recall\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Printed in large type\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Free to purchase\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Located nearby\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The phrase describes information remaining available in memory over time.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"What can be inferred about an easy study session?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It must involve retrieval\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It guarantees long-term learning\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It requires no feedback\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It may give a misleading impression of learning\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"Ease and familiarity alone do not demonstrate lasting learning.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"How is the second paragraph organized?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"A historical event followed by dates\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"A benefit followed by a condition for its success\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Two unrelated definitions\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"A list of examination rules\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The author explains the benefit of identifying gaps, then adds the need for accurate feedback.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 2,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Students often prepare for an examination by reading their notes repeatedly. This can make the material feel familiar, but familiarity is not the same as being able to recall an idea without help. An alternative approach asks students to close their notes and attempt to explain what they remember. This process is known as retrieval practice.\n\nRetrieval can be useful even when the first attempt is incomplete. After answering, a learner checks the original material and identifies gaps. The comparison provides a clearer guide for further study than the general feeling that a page looks familiar. However, feedback matters: repeatedly producing an incorrect answer without checking it may strengthen the error rather than correct it.\n\nSpacing these attempts over several days adds another challenge. Some forgetting occurs between sessions, so recall requires effort again. Although this can feel less comfortable than immediate repetition, it helps reveal whether knowledge remains accessible over time. Effective study therefore involves both attempting to retrieve information and using reliable feedback to refine it, rather than judging success solely by how easy a study session feels.",
                            Title = "When retrieval improves learning",
                            Version = 3
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("911d2255-f075-41eb-5140-b11f2cd96377"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What is the main idea?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Gold is always more informative than pottery\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Chemical tests eliminate uncertainty\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Pottery and its context together reveal past activities\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Broken objects should be discarded\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"Both the material and the context of its discovery provide evidence about past lives.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"What might chemical analysis reveal?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"The name of the pot\\u0027s owner\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Food or drink residues\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"The exact date of every scratch\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"The value of gold\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The second paragraph explicitly mentions food and drink residues.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"Why are possible alternative explanations mentioned?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To show why several observations are needed\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To reject all chemical testing\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To prove every pot was buried deliberately\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To explain why records are unnecessary\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"Different possible causes of residues and scratches show why a single observation is insufficient.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"The word \\u0027these\\u0027 in \\u0027these relationships\\u0027 refers to\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Chemical substances\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Improved methods\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Golden ornaments\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Connections between vessels and their surroundings\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"The preceding sentence describes different uses of vessels in homes and shared spaces.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"Where would this sentence best fit? \\u2018Neither kind of evidence offers a complete story on its own.\\u2019 Choose a marked location [A]\\u2013[D] in the passage.\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Location [A]\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Location [B]\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Location [C]\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Location [D]\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"Neither kind refers to chemical residues and surface marks. Position [B] follows their introduction; the next sentences explain why neither gives a definite conclusion alone.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 3,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "A broken cooking pot may appear less impressive than a golden ornament, yet it can provide rich evidence about everyday life. Pottery survives in many environments, and even small fragments can preserve traces of how a vessel was made and used. Researchers examine both the material itself and the place where it was discovered.\n\n[A] Chemical analysis may reveal residues left by food or drink. Marks on the surface can suggest repeated contact with fire or particular tools. [B] A substance could have entered the pot after it was discarded, while a scratch might have occurred during burial. [C] Researchers therefore compare several independent observations before proposing an explanation.\n\n[D] Location is equally important. Similar vessels found in homes and in communal spaces may have served different purposes. If an object is removed without a record of its surroundings, these relationships can be lost. Careful documentation makes it possible for later researchers to test earlier interpretations using improved methods. Preserving information about an object's context can consequently be as valuable as preserving the object itself.",
                            Title = "The evidence inside pottery",
                            Version = 3
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("93e23019-d9ed-83e2-ab87-78112f7883af"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What does the passage explain?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Why all islands have identical habitats\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"How isolation and area influence species communities\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Why birds cannot transport seeds\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"How farmland increases every population\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The passage connects distance, area, and habitat fragments on land.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"Why can small populations be vulnerable?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"They always move too far\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"They have too many habitats\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"A single event may affect a large part of them\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"They cannot use nesting sites\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The example of a storm destroying a single nesting site illustrates this risk.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"What does \\u0027local disappearances\\u0027 mean?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Loss from a particular place\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Extinction across the whole planet\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Seasonal changes in weather\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"The formation of new islands\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The second paragraph distinguishes disappearance from one island from global extinction.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"Why is a forest surrounded by farms compared to an island?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Both are always surrounded by water\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Both have no birds\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Both contain only one habitat\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"The surrounding area may prevent movement\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"A forest is functionally isolated for species unable to cross the surrounding farmland.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"What is implied about wildlife corridors?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"They benefit every species equally\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Their usefulness depends on the organism\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"They always eliminate local loss\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"They are unrelated to movement\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The usefulness of a corridor depends on how the particular species moves.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 4,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "The number of species on an island reflects a changing balance between arrivals and local disappearances. An island close to a mainland is generally easier for organisms to reach than a distant one. Seeds may travel on wind or birds, while some animals can cross short stretches of water. Distance therefore influences the rate at which new populations become established.\n\nArea also matters. Larger islands often contain more types of habitat and can support larger populations. Small populations are more vulnerable to chance events, such as a storm destroying the only nesting site used by a species. A disappearance from one island does not necessarily mean that the species has become extinct everywhere.\n\nThese ideas have been applied to habitat fragments on land. A forest surrounded by farms is not literally an island, but species that cannot cross farmland may experience it as one. Creating connections between fragments can make movement easier. Nevertheless, a corridor that benefits one species may not help another. Conservation planning must consider how particular organisms move, rather than assuming that every strip of vegetation provides the same connection.",
                            Title = "Why some islands have fewer species",
                            Version = 3
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("2ccdfc6f-d8ea-ca61-5ef5-538a83551ca2"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What is the passage mainly concerned with?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Using lake sediment to investigate past environments\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Predicting the exact date of future fires\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Removing all sediment from lakes\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Showing that pollen is useless\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The passage explains how layers reveal environmental history and the limits of interpretation.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"What is generally true of deeper sediment?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It contains no pollen\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It is always grass\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It is older\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It has no environmental value\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The first paragraph states that deeper material is generally older.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"Why might an increase in grass pollen be difficult to interpret?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Grass never produces pollen\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"More than one process could explain it\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Trees cannot grow near lakes\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"All pollen has the same age\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"Climate change and human land clearing can produce the same pattern.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"The word \\u0027indicator\\u0027 is closest in meaning to\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"instruction\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"container\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"obstacle\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"sign\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"Indicator means a sign or clue about past conditions.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"Why does the author contrast sediment with a precise diary?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To claim scientists should use diaries only\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To emphasize that interpretation involves uncertainty\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To argue that deeper layers are always younger\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To dismiss historical records\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"Uncertainty about dates and causes prevents reading sediment as a precise diary.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 5,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Material gradually settles on the bottoms of lakes, forming layers of sediment. These layers may contain pollen, mineral particles, and remains of small organisms. By removing a narrow column of sediment, researchers can examine a sequence in which deeper material is generally older than material near the surface. The column acts as a partial record of the surrounding environment.\n\nPollen offers clues about plants that grew nearby. If pollen from trees becomes less common while grass pollen increases, the landscape may have become more open. However, the change could reflect either a shift in climate or human activities such as clearing land. A single indicator rarely identifies the cause with certainty.\n\nResearchers strengthen interpretations by comparing pollen with other evidence, including charcoal particles and historical records. Charcoal may indicate burning, though it does not reveal by itself whether the fire was natural or deliberately started. Establishing the age of each layer also involves uncertainty. Rather than treating sediment as a precise diary, scientists combine several lines of evidence to build and revise explanations of environmental change over time.",
                            Title = "A record written in lake sediment",
                            Version = 3
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("cccfb12e-890f-4eca-87df-c324dc0312af"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What is the passage mainly about?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Why seed banks no longer need field research\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"How to grow plants without water\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Why seed preservation requires ongoing management\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Why all seeds should be frozen immediately\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"Each paragraph explains a component of managing a collection beyond simply keeping it cold.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"Why do staff test small samples?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To identify the coldest room\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To remove all genetic variation\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To calculate the weight of each packet\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To check whether stored seeds can still grow\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"Germination tests indicate whether the seeds can produce seedlings.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"What does \\u0022renewal\\u0022 refer to?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Producing a fresh supply of seeds\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Replacing written records\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Cooling an empty container\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Moving packets between rooms\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The preceding sentence describes growing stored seeds into plants that produce new ones.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"What can be inferred about using very few parent plants?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"The collection will last indefinitely\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Some original genetic variety may be lost\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"All seeds will become resistant to drying\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Documentation becomes unnecessary\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"A handful of plants may not represent the variety in the original population.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"Why does the author discuss a packet with no origin information?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To show that germination tests are unreliable\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To explain how cooling changes plant names\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To illustrate the importance of documentation\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To recommend discarding every old packet\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The example links useful research material to reliable records.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 6,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Seed banks preserve plant varieties by keeping seeds under controlled conditions. Cooling can slow the chemical reactions associated with aging, but temperature is only one part of the process. Many seeds must also be dried carefully before storage. Too much moisture can damage them, while some species cannot tolerate substantial drying at all.\n\nFor seeds that respond well to dry, cold storage, a collection is still not a permanent solution. Staff periodically remove small samples and test whether they germinate. If too few seedlings emerge, the remaining seeds may need to be grown into plants that produce a fresh supply. This renewal has to be planned carefully: growing only a handful of plants could fail to preserve the genetic variety present in the original collection.\n\nRecords are another essential component. A packet without reliable information about its origin may have limited research value. Knowing where and under what conditions the parent plants grew helps researchers select material for later studies. Thus, a successful seed bank is more than a cold room filled with containers. It is an active system of storage, testing, renewal, and documentation. Different species require different strategies, and even apparently stable collections need continuing attention.",
                            Title = "Why stored seeds need more than cold",
                            Version = 3
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("4774fda7-53c8-4543-8077-e819d1b464a0"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What central claim does the author make?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Pauses always indicate recording faults\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Longer silence is always more effective\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Musical patterns have no effect on listeners\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Silence can contribute to musical meaning\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"The passage explains anticipation and phrase separation as functions of silence.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"Why compare silence at the beginning and after a familiar sequence?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To show that prior context changes its effect\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To prove all recordings should begin silently\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To rank instruments by loudness\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To show listeners ignore familiar patterns\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The examples hold silence length constant but change what the listener has heard.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"The word \\u0022anticipation\\u0022 is closest in meaning to\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"exhaustion\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"expectation\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"confusion\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"measurement\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"A familiar pattern creates an expectation about the next sound.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"Why keep other features similar in an experiment?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To eliminate pauses from the recording\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To test which equipment is most expensive\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To isolate the effect of pause placement\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To make every listener choose the same answer\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"Matched passages reduce alternative explanations for different responses.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"What can be inferred about a technical interruption?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It necessarily improves anticipation\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It has the same effect as every planned pause\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It makes contextual information irrelevant\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It may draw attention away from musical structure\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"Listeners may focus on faulty equipment instead of the composition.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 7,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "A pause in music may seem to be an absence of activity. Yet listeners often experience silence as an important part of a musical phrase. After a repeated pattern establishes an expectation, a brief pause can delay its completion. When the expected sound finally arrives, the delay can make it feel especially significant.\n\nThis effect depends partly on what has already been heard. A silence at the beginning of an unfamiliar recording offers few clues about what will follow. The same length of silence after a familiar sequence may create strong anticipation. Duration alone therefore does not explain the experience; the surrounding pattern matters. Musicians can also use pauses to separate phrases, giving listeners time to recognize one unit before another begins.\n\nResearchers who study these effects must distinguish musical expectation from practical interruptions. If a recording suddenly stops because of a technical fault, listeners may attend to the equipment rather than the composition. In experiments, researchers can present several carefully matched versions of a passage, altering where a pause occurs while keeping other features similar. Differences in listeners' responses then help reveal how timing contributes to musical structure. Silence, in this view, is not merely empty space between notes but a feature whose meaning emerges from its context.",
                            Title = "The hidden work of a musical pause",
                            Version = 3
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("abf3dfbd-c502-4fc3-8d30-d2dd9ced8a75"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"Which approach does the passage favor?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Organizing signs around users\\u0027 decisions\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Making every sign brighter than its neighbors\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Displaying all instructions at the entrance\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Removing differences between all destinations\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The text emphasizes hierarchy, relevant locations, consistency and visitor observation.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"Why is a transport station used as an example?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"To explain how stations are constructed\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"To show that information is needed in stages\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"To compare train and bus prices\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"To argue against payment machines\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"Visitors first find an area, then a machine, then instructions.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"What does \\u0022burden\\u0022 refer to?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"The cost of bright displays\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"The distance between buildings\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"The difficulty of processing too much information\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"The weight of a ticket machine\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"The preceding sentences describe too many instructions competing at the entrance.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"What is one benefit of consistent sign design?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It removes every need to read\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It guarantees identical destinations\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It makes visitor testing unnecessary\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"People can recognize a learned visual pattern\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"Visitors can learn the pattern and locate similar information elsewhere.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"What is implied about designers?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Their familiarity may hide problems newcomers experience\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"They always prefer dim signs\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"They cannot learn from observation\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"They should avoid all visual differences\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"A designer knows the building and conventions that a newcomer has not learned.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 8,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Designers often make a sign larger or brighter when they want it to be noticed. This strategy can work when the surrounding environment is visually simple. In a crowded setting, however, many signs compete for attention using the same technique. Adding another bright display may contribute to the competition without making useful information easier to find.\n\nOne alternative is to organize information into a clear hierarchy. A visitor entering a transport station may first need to identify the ticket area, then select a particular machine, and finally read payment instructions. Displaying every instruction at the entrance gives equal visual weight to information needed at different moments. Placing relevant details at the point where a decision is made can reduce this burden.\n\nConsistency also supports navigation. If signs for the same kind of destination share a recognizable design, visitors can learn the pattern and search for it elsewhere. This does not mean every sign should look identical: important differences still need to be visible. Instead, a consistent system helps people predict where to find particular information. Evaluating such a system requires observing actual visitors. A sign that appears obvious to its designer may confuse a newcomer who does not yet understand the building or its conventions.",
                            Title = "When a brighter sign attracts less attention",
                            Version = 3
                        },
                        new
                        {
                            Id = new Guid("b1e13889-5cf7-45d9-889f-1a863896cf8f"),
                            Format = "article",
                            ItemsJson = "[{\"Id\":1,\"Prompt\":\"What is the main idea?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"An unexpected result invalidates all scientific research\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"Differences between models and observations can guide improvements\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Useful models must contain every real-world detail\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Laboratory rivers always predict sediment accurately\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The example shows how a mismatch identifies a missing influence and leads to revision.\"},{\"Id\":2,\"Prompt\":\"What feature might explain the unexpected sediment pattern?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"The color of the measuring tools\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"A complete absence of flowing water\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"Plants along the riverbank\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"A change in the laboratory building\"}],\"Answer\":\"C\",\"Explanation\":\"Vegetation can alter flow but was omitted from the channel model.\"},{\"Id\":3,\"Prompt\":\"The word \\u0022approximation\\u0022 is closest in meaning to\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"exact historical record\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"complete rejection\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"unrelated measurement\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"simplified representation\"}],\"Answer\":\"D\",\"Explanation\":\"The revised model includes a representation of plants without reproducing every detail.\"},{\"Id\":4,\"Prompt\":\"What can researchers conclude if the revised prediction improves?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"Vegetation mattered in the situation studied\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"All other omitted features are unimportant\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"The model is correct for every river\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"Weather can never influence sediment\"}],\"Answer\":\"A\",\"Explanation\":\"The text explicitly limits the finding to the setting and does not dismiss other omissions.\"},{\"Id\":5,\"Prompt\":\"How is the second paragraph related to the first?\",\"Prefix\":\"\",\"MissingLetters\":0,\"Options\":[{\"Id\":\"A\",\"Text\":\"It lists all features every model must include\"},{\"Id\":\"B\",\"Text\":\"It illustrates how an omission can become significant\"},{\"Id\":\"C\",\"Text\":\"It argues that simplification is always impossible\"},{\"Id\":\"D\",\"Text\":\"It changes the topic to river tourism\"}],\"Answer\":\"B\",\"Explanation\":\"The first paragraph discusses relevance of omissions; the second supplies a concrete case.\"}]",
                            Module = "academic-passage",
                            Position = 9,
                            PublicationStatus = 2,
                            Text = "Scientific models simplify complex systems so that particular relationships can be studied. A model of a river, for example, may represent flowing water and movable sediment while leaving out vegetation and changes in weather. Such omissions do not automatically make the model useless. Its value depends on the question being investigated and on whether the omitted features matter for that question.\n\nUnexpected results can help researchers identify these limits. Suppose a laboratory channel predicts that sediment will gather at a bend, but observations from a real river show less accumulation. Rather than immediately rejecting every part of the model, researchers can examine the difference. Plants along the real riverbank might change the flow in a way the simplified channel does not represent.\n\nA revised model could then include an approximation of those plants. If its predictions improve, this provides evidence that vegetation was important in that setting. It does not establish that every other omitted feature is irrelevant. Models are therefore often developed through repeated comparison with observations. A mismatch can be productive because it suggests what needs further investigation. The aim is not to reproduce every detail of reality, but to build a representation that is informative for a clearly stated purpose.",
                            Title = "A useful mistake in a laboratory model",
                            Version = 3
                        });
                });

